<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · mountain</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ahaggar Mountains</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Hoggar Mountains</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Ahaggar, or Hoggar, Mountains are a highland of volcanic peaks and plateaus in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algeria">Algeria</a>, in the central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sahara">Sahara</a>. They are the homeland of the Kel Ahaggar <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tuareg">Tuareg</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tamanrasset">Tamanrasset</a> is the main town. Mount Tahat, the highest peak in Algeria, rises to about 2,900 metres.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ahaggar-mountains.jpg" alt="Ahaggar Mountains" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Mohammed Amri · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%D8%A7%D8%B3%D9%83%D8%B1%D8%A7%D9%85_2_-_%D8%AA%D9%85%D9%86%D8%B1%D8%A7%D8%B3%D8%AA.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The Hoggar Mountains lie in the central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sahara">Sahara</a> in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algeria">Algeria</a>, along the Tropic of Cancer, and cover about 550,000 square kilometres, roughly 1,500 kilometres south of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algiers">Algiers</a>. The area is largely rocky desert with an average elevation above 900 metres. The mountains are mainly metamorphic rock about 2 billion years old, with younger rock where volcanic activity has laid down new material. Several of the more dramatic peaks, such as Ilamen, are extinct volcano domes worn away by erosion, which left the more resistant rock that plugged the cones. The highest peak, Mount Tahat, is 2,908 metres high.</p><p>Summers are hot and winters cold, with temperatures below freezing on winter nights, and rain is rare and sporadic. Because the climate is less extreme than in most of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sahara">Sahara</a>, the range is a major location of biodiversity, including a number of relict species. The highlands are believed to be one of the main sources of the Tamanrasset River, an ancient river that flowed during the African humid period.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Rock paintings show that people settled here in prehistory, with dates as early as 6000 BCE. The massif is the land of the Kel Ahaggar Tuareg. The tomb of Tin Hinan, the woman believed to be the matriarch of the Tuareg, is at Abalessa, an oasis near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tamanrasset">Tamanrasset</a>. In 1911 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-de-foucauld">Charles de Foucauld</a> built a hermitage at Assekrem, on the plateau at the top of the range, which a few Catholic monks still inhabit. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> conducted underground atomic tests in the mountains in the 1960s.</p><p>Some wildlife has gone from the region. A population of African wild dogs just west of the range survived into the 20th century, and West African crocodiles persisted in the mountains until the early 20th century.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The mountains are home to the Ahaggar Cultural Park, called the Ahaggar National Park from 1987 to 2011, which covers about 450,000 square kilometres and holds Mount Tahat. Its vegetation includes Vachellia tortilis, myrtle and Tamarix aphylla. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/barbary-coast">Barbary</a> sheep and Dorcas gazelles live there, and BirdLife International has designated the park an Important Bird Area.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Hoggar Mountains” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In antiquity the Tuareg moved south from the Tafilalt into the Sahel under their founding queen Tin Hinan, who is believed to have lived in the 4th or 5th century; her monumental tomb at Abalessa in the <b>Hoggar Mountains</b> bears traces of a Tifinagh inscription.</blockquote><a href="/culture/tuareg">Tuareg · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>The Kel <b>Ahaggar</b> took shape as a confederation in the Ahaggar massif of the central Sahara, with noble drum groups, especially the Kel Rela, providing the amenokal.</blockquote><a href="/state/kel-ahaggar">Kel Ahaggar</a></li></ul></section>
